# capital.com DEMO credentials. Create the key in the demo account's API settings —
# a live key will not work here, because the module refuses to start outside demo.
CAPITAL_API_KEY=
CAPITAL_IDENTIFIER=your-login-email
CAPITAL_PASSWORD=

# A second key, and the question it exists to answer: does a session opened with one key
# invalidate a session opened with another, under the same login? What is actually known
# is narrower than the rule usually quoted — logging in with *the same* credentials kills
# the previous session, which is why two gateway processes on one account deauthenticate
# each other (`client.py`). Whether two keys are two sessions has never been measured.
#
# It matters because of what it saves. If two keys are independent, `live` tests and CI
# stop needing a whole second registration (its own e-mail, its own login) to run without
# breaking production — a second key under this login is enough.
#
# The measurement, once the key is here: sign in with this key while the session from
# CAPITAL_API_KEY is alive, then call GET /session with the first key. Anything other
# than 401 means they are independent. Nothing reads this variable yet; it is a slot.
CAPITAL_API_KEY_SECONDARY=

# Both endpoints are checked at startup and must match exactly. They are here to be
# read, not changed — any other value refuses to start.
CAPITAL_BASE_URL=https://demo-api-capital.backend-capital.com
CAPITAL_STREAM_URL=wss://api-streaming-capital.backend-capital.com/connect

# This module's own caller key — not a capital.com credential. Every caller (today, only
# market-data) presents it in the X-Gateway-Key header on every request and at WebSocket
# handshake; without it, or with the wrong value, the module answers 401 before touching
# capital.com. The one exception is GET /, which the hosting platform polls to check the
# process is alive.
#
# Any value works for local development as long as it matches the same variable in
# modules/market-data/.env. In Azure this comes from Key Vault, not from a file on disk —
# see docs/azure-infrastructure-proposal.html.
GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# Set to exactly "production" in the deployed environment. Off production the module
# publishes /docs and /openapi.json (terminal's contract generation reads market-data's
# schema, not this one, so leaving this unset never affects that); on production it does
# not, because a published schema hands anyone the exact shape of /orders.
GATEWAY_ENV=
